  "live_records": {
    "q-344350": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344350",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196324/assets/dbf58b1f0399bfc16e2b38cf39566d0eb3a75e8a0a187d4157fa4bcc72a7dece.png)\n\nFind the area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$ of the largest rectangle that can be inscribed in the closed region bounded by the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the graph of $y=36-3{x}^{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{max}}=$\n {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let $x$ and $y$ be the width and height of the rectangle, respectively. Given a width $x,$ the height $y$ of the rectangle rises up to the curve $y=36-3{x}^{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize the area of the rectangle, which is given by\n\n$$\nA=xy=x(36-3{x}^{2})=36x-3{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the critical points. Taking the derivative of the area, we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=36-9{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 36-9{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $x=2$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(x).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,2\\sqrt{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $3$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $x=2$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting rectangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$ that a rectangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{max}}=A(2) & =36(2)-3(2)^{3} \\\\  & =72-24 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
