      "source_example_id": "e-356",
      "knowledge_point": "Maximizing the Area of a Rectangle Inscribed in the First Quadrant Under a Given Curve",
      "kp_id": "e-356"
    },
    "q-344351": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344351",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196324/assets/5bb4bc623941bc502806e9883b65360670f3d15f8a96fd39a738e31d909431a0.png)\n\nFind the area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$ of the largest rectangle that can be inscribed in the closed region bounded by the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the graph of $y=18-{x}^{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let $x$ and $y$ be the width and height of the rectangle, respectively. Given a width $x,$ the height $y$ of the rectangle rises up to the curve $y=18-{x}^{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize the area of the rectangle, which is given by\n\n$$\nA=xy=x(18-{x}^{2})=18x-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the critical points. Taking the derivative of the area, we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=18-3{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 18-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =6 \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $x=\\sqrt{6}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(x).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,\\sqrt{6})$ | $\\sqrt{6}$ | $(\\sqrt{6},3\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $4$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $x=\\sqrt{6}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting rectangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$ that a rectangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{max}}=A(\\sqrt{6}) & =18(\\sqrt{6})-{(\\sqrt{6})}^{3} \\\\  & =18\\sqrt{6}-6\\sqrt{6} \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
