    "q-344371": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344371",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$ of the largest rectangle that can be inscribed in the closed region bounded by the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the graph of $y=35-5{x}^{6}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{max}}=$\n {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's make a diagram of the situation:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196324/assets/7595abcc3939c90d84bdcf93e7a773386b6392ea24a48cc0d4daeb9a2e579b65.png)\n\nLet $x$ and $y$ be the width and height of the rectangle, respectively. Given a width $x,$ the height $y$ of the rectangle rises up to the curve $y=35-5{x}^{6}.$\n\nWe want to maximize the area of the rectangle, which is given by\n\n$$\nA=xy=x(35-5{x}^{6})=35x-5{x}^{7}.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the critical points. Taking the derivative of the area, we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=35-35{x}^{6},\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 35-35{x}^{6} & =0 \\\\ 35{x}^{6} & =35 \\\\ {x}^{6} & =1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $x=1$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(x).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\sqrt[6]{7})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0.5$ | $$ | $1.2$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $x=1$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting rectangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$ that a rectangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{max}}=A(1) & =35(1)-5(1)^{7} \\\\  & =35-5 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
