    "q-344541": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344541",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196324/assets/d31279386feab4e172f47f113bc330a5f7675ef460ab55c73ed6d3e8965549db.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$3\\sqrt{3},$ as shown above. Find the greatest possible perimeter ${P}_{\\text{max}}$ of the triangle. Do not round the answer.\n\n${P}_{\\text{max}}=$\n {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\nP(l)=6\\sqrt{3}+l+\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $6\\sqrt{3},$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<6\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{(-2l)}{\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =108-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =54 \\\\ l & =3\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=3\\sqrt{6}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,3\\sqrt{6})$ | $3\\sqrt{6}$ | $(3\\sqrt{6},6\\sqrt{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ | $$ | $9$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=3\\sqrt{6}$ gives the maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter ${P}_{\\text{max}}$ that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{\\text{max}}=P(3\\sqrt{6}) & =6\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{108-54} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{54} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}+6\\sqrt{6} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
