    "q-344377": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344377",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196324/assets/6763facb51594591ad017f9a792ead325acf213ea30ca092e175fb0ba8d73683.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$\\frac{1}{3},$ as shown above. Find the greatest possible perimeter ${P}_{\\text{max}}$ of the triangle. Do not round the answer.\n\n${P}_{\\text{max}}=$\n {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\nP(l)=\\frac{2}{3}+l+\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}-{l}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $\\frac{2}{3},$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{(-2l)}{\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}-{l}^{2}} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ l & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ gives the maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter ${P}_{\\text{max}}$ that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{\\text{max}}=P\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}) & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}-\\frac{2}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
