    "q-344552": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344552",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Consider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$8.$ If the hypotenuse of the triangle lies along the diameter of the semicircle, what is the greatest possible perimeter ${P}_{\\text{max}}$ of the triangle? Do not round the answer.\n\n${P}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196324/assets/538c49b44e4acba74ce5a55e93f67d06f0dc0a0d893f817659739cec5d34d020.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =16+l+\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $16,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<16.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{(-2l)}{\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}} & =l \\\\ 256-{l}^{2} & ={l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =256 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =128 \\\\ l & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=8\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,8\\sqrt{2})$ | $8\\sqrt{2}$ | $(8\\sqrt{2},16)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ | $$ | $12$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=8\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter ${P}_{\\text{max}}$ that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{\\text{max}}=P(8\\sqrt{2}) & =16+8\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{256-(8\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =16+8\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{256-128} \\\\  & =16+8\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{128} \\\\  & =16+8\\sqrt{2}+8\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =16+16\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =16(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
