    "q-84642": {
      "dom_id": "step-q84642",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196324/assets/bd18fad0e9476915901aac297267b8d3cd652f35da0ab00325bf37ea38b17043.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$2\\sqrt{2},$ as shown above. What is the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle?",
      "worked_solution": "Let's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{32-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =4\\sqrt{2}+l+\\sqrt{32-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $4\\sqrt{2},$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<4\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{32-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{32-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{32-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{32-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{32-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =32-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =32 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =16 \\\\ l & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=4$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,4\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $5$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo $l=4$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =4\\sqrt{2}+4+\\sqrt{32-{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}+4+\\sqrt{32-16} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}+4+\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}+4+4 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}+8 \\\\  & =4(2+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
