      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196324/observations/step-q118917-00c4ffe9b18397abe5af.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196324/observations/step-q118917-00c4ffe9b18397abe5af.png",
      "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/tasks/14196324/topics/1217/lesson",
      "captured_at": 1791953838.51714,
      "math_academy_id": "q-118917",
      "topic_id": "1217",
      "sequence_position": null,
      "source_step": {
        "dom_id": "step-q118917",
        "placement_id": null,
        "content_id": null,
        "type": null
      },
      "source_example_id": "e-355",
      "knowledge_point": "Maximizing the Perimeter of a Triangle Inscribed in a Circle",
      "kp_id": "e-355"
    },
    "q-344591": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344591",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196324/assets/535de188c9540c877729a72f6ed4975caaf8b10fe1b205dc12799e8380094a31.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semi-circle of radius $\\sqrt{6},$ as shown above. Find the maximum possible area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$  of the triangle. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{max}}=$ \n {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let's call $l$ the length of one leg. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{24-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\nA(l)=\\frac{1}{2}l\\sqrt{24-{l}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $2\\sqrt{6}$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<2\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $A(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(l)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{24-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{24-{l}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{24-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{24-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ 24-{l}^{2}-{l}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =12 \\\\ l & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=2\\sqrt{3}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,2\\sqrt{3})$ | $2\\sqrt{3}$ | $(2\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{6})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $4$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=2\\sqrt{3}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nFinally, the maximum possible area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{max}}=A(2\\sqrt{3}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{24-(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{24-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
